=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
The user's reported fault (Laptop2 unable to reach Laptop3 on switch1 VLAN 10) is not present: both hosts have correctly assigned addresses (172.16.10.12 and .13), their interfaces are UP, and direct-IP ICMP between them yields 0% loss. Switch1 is not dropping frames and no port is in a blocking state.

The actual network anomaly resides on router6. Its nftables FORWARD chain contains a near-total inbound block on eth2 — the interface connected to the r6_r3 link toward router3. Three rules fire in order: drop TCP (except one narrow HTTP accept for 172.16.10.0/24 → 172.18.51.1:80), drop UDP, then a catch-all drop that catches ICMP and every other protocol. The FORWARD hook means the filter applies to all traffic being routed through router6 that arrives on eth2, not just traffic destined for the router itself. The catch-all rule's counter grew from 24 to 32 packets during live testing (absorbing exactly the 8 ICMP packets from the two 4-packet cross-segment pings), conclusively confirming router6 is the drop point.

This splits the network into two unreachable halves: the router3/router4 segment (pc11–pc14 on switch5/switch6, 192.168.200.0/24) and the router6/mswitch2 segment (pc15–pc18 on switch10 and laptop4/5 on switch11, 172.18.51.0/24 and adjacent subnets). Any traffic from router3 toward router6 is silently discarded, and reply paths from the mswitch2 side back through router3 are also blocked.

Root cause: a restrictive nftables FORWARD chain on router6 drops all inbound traffic on eth2, effectively severing the r6–r3 inter-domain link for all protocols except one specific HTTP flow.

Fix: Remove the three drop rules from router6's `ip filter FORWARD` chain (`nft delete rule ip filter FORWARD handle <handle>` for each of the TCP-drop, UDP-drop, and catch-all-drop rules), retaining only the legitimate HTTP accept rule if required.
=== END ===